A class that violates both at once — and the refactor that fixes both with the same change:

```java
// Violates both: public fields (no encapsulation), and callers must
// know the internal representation (no abstraction).
class BankAccount {
    public double balance;
    public List<String> transactionLog;
}

account.balance -= 500; // any caller can corrupt state directly
```

```java
// Fixes both with one change: private fields plus an intention-revealing
// method. Callers no longer see or touch the representation.
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;
    private List<String> transactionLog;

    void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > balance) throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        balance -= amount;
        transactionLog.add("withdraw:" + amount);
    }
}
```

One refactor — private fields plus a named method — buys both properties simultaneously, which is exactly why interviewers treat them as related, not identical.
